course: dictionary
lesson: 11385
title: Abstention: choosing not to
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An abstention is a choice to stay out of something. In a class vote, a child who abstains does not put a hand up for yes or for no. «The class voted on the trip: ten for, five against, two abstentions.» The two children who did not vote made an abstention.
explain: Abstention can also mean choosing not to have something you might want. «Mia made an abstention from sweets for the whole month.» She decided to go without sweets. The word is formal, so you will meet it more in news, rules and school councils than in everyday chat.
explain: Use context to check your guess. Ask: is someone choosing not to vote, or choosing not to have something? Then swap in a simpler phrase, such as "not voting" or "giving up", and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Abstention» means choosing not to vote, neither for nor against.
fact: Formal use: «abstention» means choosing not to do or have something you might want, like food or alcohol.
fact: «Abstention» is a noun: you can have an abstention, or several abstentions.
fact: Some religions ask people to give up certain things for a time, and this giving up can also be called abstention.
exercise: «The council vote ended with ten for, four against and three abstentions.» How many people chose abstention? | *Three | Ten | Seventeen | Four
exercise: What does abstention mean in a vote? | Voting for something twice | Shouting out your answer loudly | Counting all the votes | *Choosing not to vote, neither for nor against
exercise: «Mia's abstention from sweets lasted all month.» What does abstention mean here? | Losing her sweets on the bus | Buying a new sweet shop | Eating lots of sweets | *Choosing to give up sweets
test: Sam did not vote and did not say yes or no. What did Sam make? | A raised hand | *An abstention | A complaint | A promise
test: Which best explains abstention in a class vote? | Voting for the winner | *Choosing to stay out, neither for nor against | Being the first to speak | Running for class captain
exam: Which is the best meaning of abstention in a school council? | Writing a letter to the head teacher | Shouting out a vote | Winning a debate | *Choosing not to vote, neither for nor against
